course: dictionary
lesson: 5108
title: Interrupt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To interrupt someone is to stop them while they are speaking or doing something, by speaking or doing something yourself. «If you must interrupt, you say "Excuse me" or "Sorry to interrupt" first.» Those polite words are how you cut in kindly.
explain: The word also works for things, not just people. To interrupt can mean to stop something for a short time: «Rain interrupted the match.» The match did not end for good; it paused until the rain stopped.
explain: Context tells you which meaning fits. «Taking turns makes a discussion fair; interrupting stops people being heard.» Here the people are the ones being stopped from speaking, so interrupting means cutting in while someone else is talking.
fact: Interrupt is a verb. Its forms are interrupt, interrupts, interrupted and interrupting.
fact: «Interrupt» means to stop someone while they are speaking or doing something, by speaking or acting yourself.
fact: «Interrupt» can also mean to stop something for a short time, like a match or a game.
fact: Useful phrases: «interrupt someone», «sorry to interrupt», «don't interrupt», «interrupted by».
fact: In an emergency, such as someone being hurt, it is right to interrupt an adult straight away.
exercise: «If you must interrupt, you say "Excuse me" first.» What should you say before you interrupt, to be polite? | Go away | *Sorry to interrupt | Be quiet | I'm talking now
exercise: «Rain interrupted the match.» What does interrupted mean here? | Started early | Made it louder | Ended for good | *Stopped for a short time
exercise: Ella's mum is on the phone and Ella wants to speak. What is the polite thing for Ella to do? | Take the phone away | *Wait for a pause, or put a hand gently on her arm and wait | Shout her name until she turns round | Start talking over her mum
test: What does «interrupt» mean when someone is speaking? | *To stop them by speaking or acting yourself | To leave the room quietly | To repeat everything they say | To agree with them loudly
test: Which word is the past tense of interrupt? | interrupting | interrupts | interrupter | *interrupted
exam: When is it right to interrupt an adult straight away? | *When it is urgent, like someone being hurt | When a story is getting too long | Because adults like being interrupted | Whenever you have a question
